WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:05.112
Sometimes the column you need does not exist yet, so you build it.

2
00:00:05.112 --> 00:00:09.755
In this lesson, you will learn to write a custom column formula, read the

3
00:00:09.755 --> 00:00:15.393
messages that help you fix it, set the right data type, and keep your applied steps tidy.

4
00:00:15.393 --> 00:00:16.720
Let's build it together!

5
00:00:18.600 --> 00:00:21.554
On the Add Column tab, click Custom Column.

6
00:00:21.554 --> 00:00:24.509
On the right, you see all available columns.

7
00:00:24.509 --> 00:00:28.940
Select one and click Insert, and it goes straight into your formula.

8
00:00:45.100 --> 00:00:47.531
Column names live inside square brackets.

9
00:00:47.531 --> 00:00:54.420
Miss a bracket, and Power Query tells you right away that a closing bracket is expected. Helpful!

10
00:01:06.100 --> 00:01:11.812
Here I multiply Units Sold by Manufacturing Price and by Sale Price. When you see:

11
00:01:11.812 --> 00:01:16.000
no syntax errors have been detected, you are good to go.

12
00:01:24.100 --> 00:01:26.866
I name the column Total Sale and click OK.

13
00:01:26.866 --> 00:01:29.940
Our new column appears at the end of the table.

14
00:01:46.100 --> 00:01:51.021
New custom columns often have the type Any, so always set the data type.

15
00:01:51.021 --> 00:01:52.427
I choose Decimal Number.

16
00:01:52.427 --> 00:01:57.700
Then I move Total Sale next to Sale Price, where it is easy to compare.

17
00:02:26.100 --> 00:02:28.085
Every change lives in Applied Steps.

18
00:02:28.085 --> 00:02:32.716
Right click a step, and you can rename it, delete it, insert a step

19
00:02:32.716 --> 00:02:34.370
after it, or move it.

20
00:02:34.370 --> 00:02:38.340
Give important steps clear names, so your team can follow your work.

21
00:02:56.100 --> 00:02:57.519
And the best part:

22
00:02:57.519 --> 00:03:00.357
every refresh runs these steps again, in order.

23
00:03:00.357 --> 00:03:02.840
Clean once, and reuse it every time!

